Do not call secure_getenv, so the binaries keep running on old glibc.

ossl_safe_getenv picks its implementation from the glibc of the machine doing
the build. The release is built on a system new enough to take the
secure_getenv branch, which pulls in a symbol that only exists from glibc 2.17,
while the binaries are supposed to run against 2.12 - so the build fails its own
check-symbols gate with "symbol secure_getenv from unsupported version
GLIBC_2.17" for skydoge-cli, skydoge-tx and skydoge-qt.

Take the branch below it instead, which openssl already provides for platforms
without secure_getenv and which carries the same meaning: refuse the environment
when the process is set-uid or set-gid, otherwise read it. That is stricter than
the plain getenv 1.0.1k used here, so nothing is given up by choosing it.

--- a/crypto/getenv.c
+++ b/crypto/getenv.c
@@ -15,11 +15,9 @@
 
 char *ossl_safe_getenv(const char *name)
 {
-#if defined(__GLIBC__) && defined(__GLIBC_PREREQ)
-# if __GLIBC_PREREQ(2, 17)
-#  define SECURE_GETENV
-    return secure_getenv(name);
-# endif
+#if 0
+/* secure_getenv needs glibc 2.17; the binaries must run on 2.12. */
+# define SECURE_GETENV
 #endif
 
 #ifndef SECURE_GETENV
